.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt ZPOOL-CLONEDUP 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zpool-clonedup
.Nd deduplicate a pool after the fact with block cloning
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm zpool
.Cm clonedup
.Op Fl w
.Op Fl f Ns | Ns Fl q
.Op Fl n
.Fl a Ns | Ns Ar pool Ns ...
.Nm zpool
.Cm clonedup
.Fl p
.Fl a Ns | Ns Ar pool Ns ...
.Nm zpool
.Cm clonedup
.Fl s
.Fl a Ns | Ns Ar pool Ns ...
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Finds blocks with identical content and makes them share one copy through
block cloning, the mechanism behind
.Fn copy_file_range 2
and the
.Sy block_cloning
feature
.Pq see Xr zpool-features 7 .
It keeps no deduplication table, since the Block Reference Table already
tracks how many files reference a shared block.
Between runs the pool keeps a small progress record.
It holds the transaction group the last completed run reached, shown as the
.Sy last_clonedup_txg
pool property
.Pq see Xr zpoolprops 7 ,
and the counts the next run uses to size its index.
.Pp
A run has three phases, and a counting pass may come before them.
First it walks the pool's block pointers the way a scrub does, without
reading any file data, and indexes in memory the blocks written since the
last completed run.
When
.Sy zfs_clonedup_index_filter
allows it, a counting pass walks the same block pointers first and notes how
often each checksum occurs, so that the index leaves out every block whose
checksum occurs once.
Then it walks the block pointers again and looks up every older block in
that index, which matches new data against everything already in the pool.
Finally a background apply thread reads both blocks of each match, compares
them byte for byte, and redirects one onto the other.
Unless
.Sy zfs_clonedup_apply_trust_checksum
is set, a run never clones on a checksum match alone.
.Pp
The index may use at most
.Sy zfs_clonedup_scan_mem_max
bytes, or by default a share of memory set by
.Sy zfs_clonedup_scan_mem_lim_fact
.Pq see Xr zfs 4 .
When the new data does not fit, the run splits the checksum space into
partitions and repeats the phases for each one, so memory use never exceeds
the cap.
.Pp
The run picks the surviving copy so that space is actually freed.
A block held by a snapshot cannot be freed, so it survives and the run
redirects the other copies onto it.
When snapshots hold every copy, the run still redirects the live file's
block so later snapshots share it.
The space returns when the old snapshots are destroyed.
The run prefers a block already shared by an earlier clone as the surviving
copy, because redirecting onto it adds no new reference table entry.
All-zero blocks become holes instead of clones.
.Pp
The walks share a scrub's scan code and limits:
.Sy zfs_scrub_min_time_ms
per transaction group and the scrub I/O priority on every device.
The verify reads also use the scrub priority and are not cached, and the
same in-flight byte budget that limits a scrub's reads limits them.
The kstat
.Pa zfs/ Ns Ar pool Ns Pa /clonedup
exports the run's counters, among them the blocks read to verify a match
and the number of times the apply thread stepped aside for an
administrative operation.
.Pp
A run never touches:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Snapshots, whose blocks serve as the surviving copy only.
.It
Encrypted datasets.
.It
Blocks written with
.Sy dedup Ns = Ns Sy on ,
which the deduplication table already shares.
.It
Blocks whose datasets have
.Sy clonedup Ns = Ns Sy off ,
and the files of datasets with
.Sy clonedup Ns = Ns Sy source ,
which can only be the surviving copy.
See
.Xr zfsprops 7 .
.It
Datasets in the middle of a
.Nm zfs Cm receive ,
and blocks on a device that is being removed.
.El
.Pp
Two copies of the same data stored with different compression settings have
different checksums, so a run does not find them.
In mounted filesystems and open volumes, the run rewrites blocks under the
object's range lock.
The apply thread owns an unmounted filesystem or a closed volume while it
writes to it, the way a mount or a volume open owns a dataset.
An operation that needs a dataset the thread holds, such as
.Nm zfs Cm destroy ,
.Nm zfs Cm promote ,
.Nm zfs Cm rollback ,
.Nm zfs Cm receive ,
.Nm zfs Cm mount
or opening a volume, asks the thread to let go and waits at most
.Sy zfs_clonedup_yield_timeout_ms
for it.
After letting go, the thread holds nothing until the operation has
completed.
.Pp
Redirecting a block gives it a new logical birth time.
For an incremental
.Nm zfs Cm send ,
redirecting a block written since the last snapshot adds nothing to the
stream, which carries that block anyway.
Redirecting a block that an earlier snapshot already holds makes the stream
carry it again.
To avoid that, run
.Nm zpool Cm clonedup
before taking the snapshot that will be sent, or set
.Sy clonedup Ns = Ns Sy source
on replicated datasets.
A redirected block also counts as a change to its dataset since the last
snapshot, so an incremental receive into that dataset needs
.Fl F .
Set
.Sy clonedup Ns = Ns Sy source
or
.Sy off
on datasets that receive incremental streams to avoid this.
.Pp
Only one scan runs at a time.
A scrub or resilver request cancels a running clonedup.
The next run repeats the canceled work, since
.Sy last_clonedup_txg
advances only when a run completes.
Among clonedup runs, a full run
.Pq Fl f
replaces a running quick
.Pq Fl q
or default run, and a default run replaces a quick one.
A request of equal or lower rank fails with EBUSY.
While a
.Nm zfs Cm receive Fl k
or
.Fl K
pass is running, a request to start a run fails with EBUSY.
.Pp
A run needs the
.Sy clonedup
and
.Sy block_cloning
features enabled, and
.Sy zfs_bclone_enabled
must not be zero.
Of the two features, a dry run needs only
.Sy block_cloning .
The
.Sy clonedup
feature is active only while a run is in progress.
After an export or a crash, a run begins its current partition again, and
the counts it reports from then on cover only the partitions after the
restart.
.Pp
On a pool that only receives streams, every block is held by a snapshot.
A run there redirects the head's copy of each duplicate and reports the
space as held by snapshots, which changes every dataset since its last
snapshot: the next incremental receive needs
.Fl F ,
and its rollback discards the redirects.
On such a pool,
.Nm zfs Cm receive Fl K
runs the pass on the received blocks before the snapshot is taken
.Pq see Xr zfs-receive 8 .
.Bl -tag -width "-a"
.It Fl a
Run on all pools.
.It Fl f
Index every block instead of only those written since the last completed
run, and skip the matching walk.
Only a full run finds duplicates among data that existed before the last
run.
.It Fl n
Dry run.
Perform every phase but clone nothing, and report what a real run would have
saved.
A dry run leaves no record in the pool: no scan state, no history entry and
no feature activation.
It exists only in memory, so an export or a reboot ends it, and
.Nm zpool Cm status
then reports nothing about it.
It does not advance
.Sy last_clonedup_txg .
.It Fl p
Pause a running clonedup.
To resume it, run
.Nm zpool Cm clonedup
again with the
.Fl f ,
.Fl q
and
.Fl n
flags it was started with.
A request with other flags is ranked against the paused run as described
above.
.It Fl q
Quick.
Index the new blocks and clone duplicates among them only, without matching
them against older data.
A quick run does not advance
.Sy last_clonedup_txg ,
so the next run without this flag matches the same blocks against older
data.
.It Fl s
Stop a running clonedup.
.It Fl w
Wait until the run has completed before returning.
.El
.Pp
.Xr zpool-status 8
shows progress and results, and
.Xr zpool-wait 8
can wait for the
.Sy clonedup
activity.
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Example 1 : No Deduplicating data written since the last run
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm clonedup Fl w Ar tank
.
.Ss Example 2 : No Estimating the space a full run would reclaim
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm clonedup Fl f Fl n Fl w Ar tank
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm status Ar tank
.
